  "markdown_report": "The best first step is a bounded, paired diagnostic adapter with a preregistered falsification experiment testing whether its measurements help predict ranking improvements. Preserve SASRec and the benchmark. No ranking improvement or diagnostic predictive validity has yet been measured.\n\n**Insights from the starting point**\n\n1. Broad relevance can obscure specific interests. Recent and repeated interactions provide observable evidence, but cannot establish current intent, ownership, or dislikes.\n2. Existing margins depend on score scale and negative sampling. Paired ranks improve interpretability, but remain candidate-dependent and do not establish generalization.\n3. Stable encoding and scoring hooks support behavioral probes. Position-preserving deletion additionally requires architecture-specific attention masking; unavailable metadata cannot support compatibility conclusions.\n\n**Insights from related work**\n\n1. Excessive recency bias can reduce accuracy [1]; removal sensitivity does not imply that invariance is desirable [4]. Compare recent, older, and random controls.\n2. Sampled metrics can reverse model comparisons [2]. Fixed candidates ensure paired reproducibility, while repeated validation reuse still risks selection bias [5].\n3. SASRec combines positional embeddings and causal attention [6]. Compacted deletion changes evidence and positions; selective prediction separately motivates abstaining from unsupported compatibility judgments [3].\n\n**Framework and directions**\n\n| Direction | Existing signal | Proposed extension | Interpretation limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ranking reliability | Raw margins | Paired ranks; deterministic ties | Candidate-dependent |\n| Temporal reliance | Unmodified histories | Matched deletion; prefix shuffles; position controls | Sensitivity, not intent |\n| Evidence quality | Qualitative feedback | Supported judgment or abstention | Requires annotation |\n| Diagnostic usefulness | Assumed relevance | Preregistered intervention predictions | Exploratory, limited power |\n\n**Candidate ideas**\n\nScores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Paired perturbation adapter with falsification — 4/7/5.** Originality: useful integration, but established components. Potential: reusable intervention screening, but predictive validity is unproved. Difficulty: existing hooks help, but position controls and reproducibility add work [1,2,4,6].\n- **Candidate-set disagreement audit — 3/7/4.** Originality: useful synthesis, but little new theory. Potential: exposes sampling dependence, but cannot replace pinned evaluation. Difficulty: sampled comparisons are easy, but catalog scans cost time [2].\n- **Evidence-backed compatibility audit — 6/6/7.** Originality: selective judgment transfers usefully, but abstention is established. Potential: explains unsupported recommendations, but annotation may not predict HR@10. Difficulty: public text suffices for some judgments, but reliable rules require checking [3].\n\n**Selected algorithm**\n\nAt 0% progress, implement the first idea incrementally. On fixed training prefixes, compare unchanged histories with equal-count recent, oldest, and seeded random deletions; separately shuffle earlier items while preserving the last item, occupied slots, and multiset. Keep candidates identical. Report paired target-rank changes, top-10 overlap, counts, per-category results, and equal-weight macro summaries. Training-target measurements remain in-sample.\n\nCompaction confounds evidence removal with position changes. Where supported, add deletion preserving survivor position IDs and masking removed keys/values in every layer; hold the final item/readout fixed. Attention normalization still changes. Shuffle dependence establishes neither useful transitions nor higher-order reasoning; unchanged scores do not establish unused history.\n\nBefore validation, freeze a small intervention list, probe-direction predictions, query cap, and GPU budget. Compare predictions with paired pinned HR@10 changes beyond training loss and recency/popularity summaries; treat associations as exploratory. Include last-item, uniform-pooling, and fixed-recency additive controls only within budget. Bootstrap users within categories; intervals exclude training-seed and adaptive-selection uncertainty. Preserve required interfaces and select checkpoints only by pinned macro validation HR@10.\n\nReferences: [1] https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722; [2] https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/; [3] https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15610; [4] https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13165; [5] https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/bad5f33780c42f2588878a9d07405083-Abstract.html; [6] https://github.com/pmixer/SASRec.pytorch/blob/master/python/model.py.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Extend DiagnosisProbe with reproducible paired history perturbations, explicit positional controls, and a small preregistered experiment testing whether diagnostic measurements help predict improvements in the pinned macro validation HR@10.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback identifies recommendations supported by broad associations rather than recent, specific evidence. Existing embedding and margin probes do not measure this dependence, but perturbation sensitivity alone cannot identify a beneficial intervention. At the initial research stage, a bounded falsification experiment can assess whether developing these diagnostics further is justified.",
    "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, official splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Store all outputs beneath the trial directory and charge training, diagnostics, debugging, and failed trials to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Access the actual trained module through view.model and use the compatibility encoding/scoring hooks. Preserve existing embedding and margin measurements and their limitations; report unavailable category metadata explicitly rather than producing category-based claims. Do not use unavailable ratings, reviews, prices, stores, item categories, or simulator traits.\n\nBefore observing intervention validation results, serialize a manifest containing diagnostic user IDs and prefix cutoffs, candidate lists, perturbation settings and seeds, intervention configurations, probe-direction predictions, checkpoint schedule, query cap, and diagnostic GPU-time cap. Construct diagnostic examples from public training histories only: one example per selected user, using the final training interaction as target and its preceding interactions as prefix. These are in-sample targets because baseline training may already have used them. Choose up to 32 eligible users per category deterministically; require at least three prefix items and at least 99 eligible negative items. Set k=min(3,max(1,floor(0.2*prefix_length)),prefix_length-1), and use five isolated shuffle/random-deletion seeds. Use stable cryptographic hashes of category, user ID, and replicate to derive RNG seeds; do not use Python's process-randomized hash or mutate global training/evaluation RNGs. Save actual selections to remove dependence on iteration order.\n\nSample 99 unique diagnostic negatives excluding padding, the target, and observed prefix items. Keep each user's candidate IDs and ordering identical across variants and checkpoints. These diagnostic exclusions are independent of the host evaluator: preserve its pinned users, 99 unique negatives, exclusions, seed 42, and ranking semantics exactly. Record exclusions and eligibility counts. For diagnostic ties, sort candidates by ascending item ID, then use a stable descending score sort; target rank is one plus the number of strictly higher scores plus equal-scoring candidates with smaller item ID. Use this same ordering for diagnostic top-10 sets. Do not assume the host uses this tie rule.\n\nRight-align compacted histories with zero left padding, preserving the baseline length L, integer dtype, and device. For ordinary SASRec use log2feats(sequence)[:, -1, :] as the final representation. Explicitly support shared candidate IDs [C] and per-user candidates [B,C], returning [B,C] scores without dropping the batch dimension when B=1. Prefix shuffles preserve the last item, occupied slots, prefix length, and item multiset. Recent/oldest/random compacted deletions remove k items from their respective pools; label recent deletion that removes the last item separately because it changes the immediate conditioning item.\n\nFor a position-controlled comparison, define a separate eligible pool excluding the last item and use the same k and deletion indices in compacted and position-preserving variants. Position-preserving removal retains survivor position IDs and masks deleted slots as attention keys/values at every layer while retaining the final item and readout position. Prevent all-masked attention rows and nonfinite outputs. Simply inserting zero IDs is insufficient unless the actual architecture masks those keys/values throughout every layer. If the compatibility hooks cannot express the required mask, report the probe as unsupported. This control reduces positional confounding but does not remove attention-renormalization or distribution-shift effects. Restore model mode in a finally block and verify unchanged-history controls reproduce scores within a recorded numerical tolerance.\n\nReport paired target-rank changes, target hit changes within the diagnostic candidates, top-10 set overlap, feature cosine similarity where defined, eligible user counts, and variation across perturbation seeds. Average replicates within users, then users within category, then the four categories equally. Define positive rank change as worse ranking and overlap as intersection size divided by 10. Do not silently omit a missing category from a four-category macro statistic. Use 1,000 paired user-bootstrap replicates within each category with an isolated seed and report percentile intervals; flag small samples and explain that these intervals exclude training-seed variability and adaptive-selection bias.\n\nFreeze a small intervention list before querying validation: unchanged SASRec, a fixed five-item input window, and uniform removal of 10% of nonfinal input tokens during training while preserving targets and positions. Only run interventions after explicitly budgeting their estimated training cost; retain the same seeds and training/checkpoint schedule. Compare diagnostic predictions against intervention-minus-baseline pinned per-category HR@10 changes, alongside training-loss and training-derived recency/popularity summaries. With few interventions and four categories, use descriptive prediction errors and sign agreement rather than fitted significance claims. Separately trained last-item, uniform-pooling, and fixed-recency additive item-to-candidate controls are useful explanatory extensions only if budget remains; freeze their settings before validation. Stop expanding probes if they show no incremental predictive value. Sensitivity is not an objective to maximize, and neither fixed candidates nor bootstrap intervals prevents adaptive validation overfitting. Make no claims of improved HR@10 until measured. The diagnostic-only phase requires no predictor changes; any subsequent architecture or inference change must also update the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads and reproduces the trained model.",
    "pseudocode": "prepare_manifest_before_intervention_validation():\n    freeze diagnostic users/prefixes, candidates, perturbation seeds and settings\n    freeze interventions, directional predictions, checkpoints, query and GPU caps\n    save manifest beneath trial directory\n\nrun_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    save view.model.training\n    try:\n        view.model.eval()\n        retain existing supported embedding and sampled-margin probes\n        for category in the four required categories:\n            for fixed training-prefix/target example in manifest:\n                candidates = saved target plus 99 unique diagnostic negatives\n                original = score(right_aligned_prefix, candidates)\n                verify unchanged-history score control\n                k = saved deletion count\n                variants = recent_k, oldest_k, random_k_deletions\n                variants += five prefix shuffles preserving last item and occupied slots\n                for variant:\n                    encode with actual trained model and documented padding\n                    score identical candidates; preserve [B,C] including B=1\n                    rank with stable descending scores and item-ID tie break\n                    record paired rank/hit change, top-10 overlap, feature similarity\n                if architecture supports explicit per-layer masks:\n                    select matched nonfinal deletion indices\n                    compare compacted and position-preserving removal\n                    keep final item/readout fixed; verify finite outputs\n                else:\n                    record positional control as unsupported\n        average replicates within user, users within category, categories equally\n        compute paired category-stratified user-bootstrap intervals\n        return {'metrics': measured_results,\n                'metric_definitions': formulas, counts, settings, and limitations}\n    finally:\n        view.model.train(saved_mode)\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    embed measured metrics and their definitions\n    request evidence-linked findings, unsupported-probe notes, and uncertainty\n    distinguish in-sample sensitivity from validation generalization\n    prohibit inferred intent, ownership, dislikes, unavailable traits, or unmeasured gains\n\nbounded_falsification_experiment():\n    train only preregistered interventions that fit remaining GPU budget\n    run pinned validation unchanged at frozen checkpoints\n    compare predicted directions with paired intervention HR@10 changes\n    report per-category and macro results, simple comparator signals, and uncertainty\n    retain selection by pinned macro validation HR@10\n    record null/contradictory findings and stop unsupported probe expansion",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Integrates architecture-aware position controls, paired ranking measurements, and a preregistered test of diagnostic usefulness within a reusable adapter.",
      "negative": "Perturbation tests, positional controls, and falsification experiments are established methods; originality is primarily in their integration."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Could provide a reusable basis for deciding which diagnostics deserve further investment across architectures and interventions.",
      "negative": "Predictive validity is unestablished, and the small intervention set provides limited evidence that diagnostic signals generalize beyond this search."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "The first stage reuses existing encoding and scoring hooks and requires no external data or pretrained models.",
      "negative": "Correct per-layer deletion masks, position handling, deterministic ranking, reproducible manifests, and budgeted intervention comparisons require more work than the original rating acknowledged."
    }
  },
